</style></div></a></div><div class="blog-post-header grid-column-2"><div class="blog-post-header_left-column"><div class="margin-bottom"><div class="w-embed"><a href="/blog?category=Threat Intel" data-wf--pill--variant="tertiary" class="pill card-paadding-small radius-round w-variant-2dc48ad7-f81f-aaa2-a89e-c7b3f8505f10"><div class="pill_text w-variant-2dc48ad7-f81f-aaa2-a89e-c7b3f8505f10">Threat Intel</div></a></div></div><div class="blog-post-header_title"><h1 class="heading-style-h4">litellm 1.107.0 Compromised on PyPI and Docker: Credential Stealer and Hourly Remote Code Loader</h1></div><div class="blog-post-header_preview"><div>litellm 1.107.0 shipped a credential stealer and an hourly remote code loader in all seven PyPI wheels and the official Docker images. The attacker hijacked BerriAI&#x27;s release workflow with a GitHub organization owner&#x27;s token stolen 11 days earlier through a leaked key that was never revoked. Full analysis, IOCs, and recovery steps.</div></div><div class="blog-post-header_footer"><div class="blog-post-header_date-author"><div class="blog-post-header_author"><div class="text-size-small no-line-height text-weight-bold">Sai Likhith</div><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sai-likhith-paradarami-323aaa171/" class="styled-link is-alt w-inline-block"><div class="visually-hidden">View LinkedIn</div><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" id="Layer_1" x="0px" y="0px" viewBox="0 0 150 150" STYLE="enable-background:new 0 0 150 150;" width="100%" fill="currentColor" class="icon-1x1-xxsmall"><g><path d="M81.2,67.4v-0.2c0,0.1-0.1,0.1-0.1,0.2H81.2z" class="st0"></path><path d="M0,11.5v126.9c0,6.4,5.2,11.5,11.5,11.5h126.9c6.4,0,11.5-5.2,11.5-11.5V11.5C150,5.2,144.8,0,138.5,0H11.5   C5.2,0,0,5.2,0,11.5z M46.7,124.6H24.4v-67h22.3V124.6z M35.5,48.5h-0.1c-7.5,0-12.3-5.1-12.3-11.6c0-6.6,5-11.6,12.6-11.6   c7.6,0,12.3,5,12.4,11.6C48.1,43.4,43.3,48.5,35.5,48.5z M126.9,124.6h-22.3V88.8c0-9-3.2-15.1-11.3-15.1c-6.2,0-9.8,4.1-11.4,8.1   c-0.6,1.4-0.7,3